Yes. That distinction is worth preserving.

“Embarrassingly literal” works because SAT’s foundational move is not to invent exotic new machinery first. It is to take the Minkowski grammar at face value: if a thing persists through time, then in 4D it has extent; if a 3D point persists, its covariant representation is a line; if a 3D bulk persists, its covariant representation is a tube or higher-dimensional extended object.

But SAT does not therefore need to endorse a metaphysical block universe. The block picture can be used as a static geometric representation of the same structure that a dynamic worldline-only or evolving-slice account would generate.

So the equivalence class is something like:

\[
\text{block representation}
\;\sim\;
\text{dynamically generated worldtube}
\;\sim\;
\text{ordered family of instantaneous slices}
\]

provided they encode the same covariant history.

That is important because it keeps the model representational rather than ontological. SAT can say: “For calculation, treat the full history as an extended geometric object,” without saying “the future literally already exists.”

And then H(s)H pushes that same literalism one step further: if the extended history already has worldtube geometry, ask whether the tube’s internal geometry—helix, throat, twist, holonomy—carries the things that particle physics usually treats as separate properties.

That is a cleaner doctrinal formulation than “SAT assumes a block universe.” It doesn’t need to. The block picture is just one convenient gauge, so to speak, on the same covariant object. memcite

